<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Sasanian Yemen</h1><p class="st-meta">570 CE – 630 CE (approx.) · capital Sanaa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Yemen as a province of the Persian Empire, ruled by a Persian garrison whose descendants, the Abna, helped bring the country into Islam.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 570 the Sasanian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a> sent a small force under the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wahriz">Wahriz</a> at the request of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayf-ibn-dhi-yazan">Sayf ibn Dhi Yazan</a> to expel the Ethiopians of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aksumite-yemen">Aksumite Yemen</a>. Sayf was set up as a client ruler, but after his murder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> took direct control, governing from Sanaa through a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marzban">marzban</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Persian rule was light and focused on Sanaa and the trade routes. Persian soldiers settled and married locally, forming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abna">Abna</a>, a distinct elite group. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Yemen</a> gave the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> a foothold on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean-trade">Indian Ocean trade</a> against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and its allies, but the provincial governors had limited reach into the tribal highlands.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> weakened after its wars with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a>, the governor Badhan accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> around 628 and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Yemen</a> came under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/islamic-state-of-medina">Islamic state of Medina</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abna">Abna</a> remained influential in early Islamic Sanaa.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sasanian-yemen.jpg" alt="Sasanian Yemen" width="320" height="188"><figcaption>Image: Original file by Ro4444, edited by me · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Sasanian_Empire_at_its_apex_under_Khosrow_II.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>570 CE – 630 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sanaa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Wahriz, Badhan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zoroastrianism (elite), Judaism, Christianity, Arabian polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Persian, Arabic, Sabaic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Yemen under the Abna</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=600&amp;focus=sasanian-yemen" aria-label="Sasanian Yemen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5887 1607 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sasanian Yemen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6207 1863L6357 1235M6207 1863L6053 1533"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6357 1235h0M6053 1533h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6207 1863h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6207 1863h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sanaa. <a href="/atlas?year=600&amp;focus=sasanian-yemen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
